<!--
    repipe-16-byte-vm-state: "16-byte VM state swaps emit as one-byte array
    assignments"
    docs/re-needs/16-byte-vm-state.md

    A VM interpreter keeps two four-word register banks in a 16-byte stack slot
    each and swaps them with a movdqa/movdqa/movaps/movaps quartet.  Both banks
    recover as `char v[16]`, and the printer rendered the sixteen-byte transfer
    `v2[0] = v3[0];` - a one-byte lvalue for a sixteen-byte copy, and a false
    statement about the program.  The vector zero-init became `v2[0] = 0;` the
    same way.

    P9 emission.  `PrintC::pushSymbolDetail` routes a symbol-mapped access
    through `pushPartialSymbol`, whose walk breaks out at the top on a whole
    symbol cover (off == 0 and size == the symbol's size) and leaves the caller
    to render.  kuna's caller has a dedicated whole-array `name[index]` branch
    computing `index = symboloff / elementAlignSize` that never consults the
    access size, so every whole-array cover printed element zero.  A PARTIAL
    multi-element access was already repaired - it reaches the walk's artificial
    `._<off>_<size>_` field - so `v.._0_4_` and `v..[0]` appeared side by side in
    one function for accesses four times apart in width.

    Fix: `option arraycoverwidth` (default on) suppresses that top-of-walk break
    for an array whose element stride is smaller than the access, so the cover
    falls to the same artificial field a partial access gets and renders
    `v2._0_16_`.  An access that fits inside one element keeps its subscript;
    scalars, structs and unions keep the upstream break.

    Source (as(1), x86-64, linked -Ttext=0x100000, `sink` at 0x100049):

        vm: sub $0x38,%rsp             ; two 16-byte banks at rsp+0 and rsp+0x10
            pxor %xmm0,%xmm0
            movaps %xmm0,(%rsp)        ; bank A = 0     (16-byte store)
            movaps %xmm0,0x10(%rsp)    ; bank B = 0     (16-byte store)
            mov %rsp,%rdi ; call sink  ; both banks escape, so nothing is dead
            lea 0x10(%rsp),%rdi ; call sink
            movdqa (%rsp),%xmm1        ; the swap, verbatim from the witness
            movdqa 0x10(%rsp),%xmm2
            movaps %xmm2,(%rsp)
            movaps %xmm1,0x10(%rsp)
            mov %rsp,%rdi ; call sink
            movzbl 0x3(%rsp),%eax      ; one genuine in-element byte read
            add $0x38,%rsp ; ret
        sink: ret

    Pass 1 (default): assertions #1-#3 prove every sixteen-byte access carries
    its width, and #4 that the one-byte read keeps its subscript in both passes.
    Pass 2 (arraycoverwidth off): assertion #5 reproduces the filed bug, and its
    max="1" is what pins the repair - the same line appears once, not twice.
-->
